NAME
       ntpd - network time synchronization daemon

SYNOPSIS
       **ntpd** [**-q**]

DESCRIPTION
       Keeps the system clock in step with NTP servers (RFC 5905 client
       mode). Listens on UDP 123 (ntp) and asks every configured server for
       the time at once. From the replies with the smallest round-trip delay
       the median offset is taken.

       The first correction, and any offset of 128 ms or more, steps the
       clock at once. Smaller offsets are slewed: the clock runs 0.05%
       faster or slower until the difference is gone, so time never jumps
       backwards. Offsets over 1000 s after the first sync are ignored as
       bogus. The hardware clock (RTC) is updated after every step and every
       11 minutes while synchronized.

       The poll interval starts at 2^minpoll seconds and grows up to
       2^maxpoll while the clock stays within 50 ms. Servers that answer
       with a DENY or RSTR kiss-o'-death code are not asked again.

       Started and restarted by netd(8). Needs root or the NET_BIND and
       SYS_TIME capabilities.

OPTIONS
       **-q**     Synchronize once and exit (0 on success).

FILES
       /etc/ntp.conf      servers and poll limits
       /run/dhcp/*.lease  "ntp=" lines from DHCP option 42
       /run/ntpd/status   current server, stratum, offset, delay, peers

EXAMPLES
       cat /run/ntpd/status
       journalctl | grep ntpd

SEE ALSO
       netd(8), date(1), ss(8)
